    "role": "assistant",
    "type": "assistant_message",
    "content": "All 16 checklist items have been tested and recorded — **13 PASS, 3 FAIL**.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe knowledge base performs well on its core document experience: module labelling and filtering, document detail rendering, reference-link navigation, collapse/expand with a rotating chevron indicator, the \"expand the section first\" guard prompt, no-results messaging, and the knowledge graph's structural fidelity (19 nodes, module-coloured, with exactly the 19 relationship edges present in the data, and no document ever referencing itself).\n\nThree defects were found:\n\n**IX-16 — Graph nodes don't open their document.** Clicking a node only marks it selected. The graph view has no document panel and doesn't switch tabs, so the user must manually click Documents to see the result — despite the nodes declaring `data-semtag-target=\"document.detail\"`.\n\n**FT-9 — Graph pan is non-functional.** Document selection, per-section expand/collapse state, and zoom (140%) all survive tab switching correctly. But panning never works. The root cause is in the SVG `onMouseDown` handler, which only begins a drag when `e.target === containerRef.current?.querySelector('svg')` — that selector returns the *first* SVG in the container, which is the zoom-in button icon, never the graph canvas. The grab cursor therefore promises an interaction that can't happen, and the pan half of the state-preservation requirement can't be exercised at all.\n\n**FT-10 — Module filter leaks into Favorites.** Favorites persist across reload, the count updates immediately, and markers survive sorting and filtering. However, with the UI filter active in Documents, the Favorites view showed only 1 of 3 favorites while the badge still read \"3\". Favorites exposes only search and sort, so the inherited filter can't be cleared from that view — the user has to go back to Documents and reselect \"All\".\n\nWorth noting for the FT-9 verdict: the tab-switching preservation itself works for everything observable. I recorded it as FAIL because pan is explicitly named in the requirement and the underlying feature is broken, which seemed more useful than passing the item and leaving the bug unreported — the detailed record distinguishes what passed from what's broken."
  }
}
